      "headline": "The Mystery Ache That Loves to Travel",
      "keyInsight": "Here is the great paradox: your aching spine is completely innocent. For decades, medicine treated back pain like a cracked car bumper. Doctors blamed herniated discs, bad posture, and heavy boxes. Yet millions of people walk around with gnarly spinal scans and zero pain. Meanwhile, people with picture-perfect spines suffer agonizing spasms. The pain is not an injury in your bone. It is an ingenious smokescreen orchestrated by your nervous system. When emotional pressure climbs, your brain needs a quick decoy. It turns on a throbbing siren in your lower back, neck, or shoulders. You obsess over orthopedic chairs, heating pads, and special cushions. While you inspect your spine, your emotional pressure escapes unseen. The pain travels around because the decoy moves whenever you stop fearing the old spot.",
