      "content": "Imagine watering a garden lawn on a hot summer afternoon. If someone steps hard on the rubber garden hose, the gushing stream drops to a pathetic trickle. The green grass underneath soon wilts from lack of water. Your autonomic nervous system can step on its own internal hoses whenever it wants. It does this automatically without asking your permission.\n\nWhen your emotional center triggers the pain decoy, it tightens microscopic blood vessels around specific muscles and nerves. This reduces local blood flow by just a few tiny percentage points. The tissues are not dying, but they run low on fresh oxygen. Without enough oxygen, muscles cramp instantly and nearby nerve fibers fire urgent pain signals back to the skull.\n\nThis creates a savage self-fulfilling loop. The sudden ache terrifies you, so you lie flat on your back and fear every movement. That panic floods your bloodstream with stress chemistry, which pinches the vessels even tighter. You treat the muscle like fragile glass, but glass was never broken. Once you understand that oxygen starvation is the culprit, the terror fades, the vessels open wide, and the pain melts away.",
